2186176868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1045 "Describing Properties of the Cosecant Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176117 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b5e5427c-d46e-5198-b0d9-c69a155fe586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176867 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176867 #edb/ref 1061 41806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176867 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8485b132-6a9a-5840-b8d5-354005537165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176867 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176868 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176868 #edb/ref 1061 41807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176868 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c8301c9-9d8d-5faf-85bd-9953f54432dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176868 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176869 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176869 #edb/ref 1061 41810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176869 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6312d083-7bf0-5033-ab8b-23dffd2c8040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176869 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176870 #edb/ref 1061 41811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02d19625-6a8c-527d-95de-e106d7abf898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176878 #edb/ref 1061 41841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176878 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cf094b8-771c-510b-a5f8-f59eca4b156e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176878 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176899 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176899 #edb/ref 1061 45527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "755ad03e-ee78-55e9-9249-6a54b86b8b45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177070 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's look at the graph of the cosecant function again.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nThe graph of the cosecant function has infinitely many asymptotes, and there is an infinite number of points where the function takes values of $-1$ and $1.$\n\nLet's write general expressions for these properties, by assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has asymptotes when $x=0,\\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots,$ etc. Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $1$ when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period of $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = 1$ is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{-1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $-1$ when $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = -1$ is\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +2n\\pi.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177070 #edb/ref 1065 6902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177070 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e01b49e-464d-573e-a389-92bab4f6161c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177070 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177071 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the graph of the function $y=\\csc x$ once more.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nAs previously mentioned, the cosecant function inherits its periodicity from the sine function, so the period is $2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce59afb77eb33880d7e92d1c294b872ec3af898c43a2af6816ef63fee64abd3b.png)\n\nThe periodicity of $y=\\csc x$ means that $\\csc (x+2n\\pi ) = \\csc x$ for any number $x$ in the domain of $\\csc x$ and any integer $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177071 #edb/ref 1065 6903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177071 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a00a960f-3462-59dc-9e77-5d7bf69c65bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177071 #edb/ref 1067 "The Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177086 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosecant is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cosecant function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb63f7c0930524e9f54a65aad6d74784c403a6255f723b879d76586ad8ea598b.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cosecant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177086 #edb/ref 1065 7902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177086 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0e094388-438b-588a-9c7a-00cb1afc6f15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177086 #edb/ref 1067 "The Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333370 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333370 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333370 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2948f95d-327e-5b2a-ae44-d052e460ef7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333715 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333715 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333715 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f47b504-2866-5cc0-a5de-126c425a1f79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333760 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "546b3906-9278-5c88-9389-7d8f880155e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334655 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334655 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334655 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c041a1ee-404c-5c71-88e2-cb1195daeaea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335102 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1045889-a661-5c2b-b9ef-ba20d8522b18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335505 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8aa416a5-c692-5dfb-b0e9-591568885d69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335824 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The domain of the function is all real numbers.\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- The graph of the function doesn't intersect the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7573ddef-f834-5ca7-911d-588bad3a2d0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335824 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The domain of the function is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi ,$ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The graph of the function doesn't intersect the $y$-axis, as $x=0$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ Hence, statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
